<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Belize Settlement</h1><p class="st-meta">1638 CE – 1862 CE (approx.) · capital Belize Town</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British logwood and mahogany cutters' settlement on the Bay of Honduras, tolerated by Spain for over a century and the origin of modern Belize.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition dates the first British settlement to 1638 and links it to the buccaneer Peter Wallace, but reliable evidence of logwood cutters, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baymen">Baymen</a>, on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/belize">Belize</a> River begins in the mid-17th century. Former buccaneers turned to cutting logwood, a valuable dye wood, on a coast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> claimed but did not occupy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> never recognised British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> but allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> cutting under the treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1763 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> in 1783 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-london-1786">Convention of London</a> of 1786. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baymen">Baymen</a> governed themselves through a Public Meeting and elected magistrates, joined from 1786 by a British superintendent. The economy moved from logwood to mahogany, worked by enslaved Africans in small forest gangs. Settlers repulsed a Spanish attack at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-st-georges-caye">Battle of St George's Caye</a> in 1798.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Central American independence, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> extended its control and the settlement's boundaries. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Guatemala</a> later claimed the territory, a dispute that still lingers. In 1862 the settlement was formally declared the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-honduras">Colony of British Honduras</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/belize-settlement.jpg" alt="Belize Settlement" width="320" height="229"><figcaption>Image: David Cox · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Cox_-_Pirate%27s_Isle_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1638 CE – 1862 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Belize Town</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>George Arthur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Belizean Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belize">Belize</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Settlement in the Bay of Honduras, Settlement of Belize</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1750&amp;focus=belize-settlement" aria-label="Belize Settlement on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2285 1528 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Belize Settlement lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2605 1784L4997 651M2605 1784L2588 1800"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M2588 1800h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2605 1784h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2605 1784h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1750&amp;focus=belize-settlement">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
